  <section id="review">
    <h2>Things to review</h2>

    <h3>Differences from the longer Circassian Center text</h3>
    <ul class="review">
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>mayor or candidate?</strong> This entry says he
          <em>stood as a candidate</em> for the mayoralty
          (<span lang="tr">«aday oldu»</span>). The longer text says he
          <em>held</em> the post (<span lang="tr">«Belediye Başkanlığı’nda bulundu»</span>),
          and Mirzoev has him as chairman of the Quneitra municipality from 1928
          to 1936. A candidacy that failed would conflict with both. It is more
          likely a loose retelling, but I can’t tell which is right from the
          text.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>studies in France.</strong> This entry says only that
          he “continued his studies” there. It leaves out the Grignon and
          Sciences Politiques schools, and the diplomas, that the longer text
          names. That keeps this version clear of the conflict with Mirzoev’s
          account (agronomist in Beirut, then the Sorbonne), but it also means
          it can’t settle it.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the school.</strong> “Played a large part in opening”
          is vaguer than the longer text’s “arranged the opening” of a
          mixed-sex, middle-school-level school. No year is given in either.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the newspaper.</strong> This entry says he “wrote
          articles” for <em>Marg</em>. The longer text says he also helped
          publish it, and gives 1928–31. Here there are no dates and no
          mention of the Marj front page issue.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>what was cut.</strong> The father and grandfather,
          the languages he spoke, the 1937 company, the Druze mediation and the
          personal details are all missing. That removes the claims I
          flagged as unsourced, but I can’t tell whether this is because the
          writer doubted them or just shortened the text.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>the works.</strong> Titles appear only in Turkish
          translation. The Arabic forms, and the “Homs 1948” and “1980s” notes,
          that conflict with Mirzoev’s Amman 1984 and Damascus 1984, are
          missing.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>“hayatını kaybetti”.</strong> Literally “lost his
          life”. It is a standard Turkish way to say someone died, and doesn’t
          by itself suggest an accident or violence.
        </mark>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Questions about the relationship between the three Turkish texts</h3>
    <ul class="review">
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>which came first?</strong> If the 2016 date is right,
          this short entry is older than the Circassian Center profile (22 March
          2019), so it is probably not a shortening of that profile. The longer
          text is older still: the
          <a href="/people/amin-samkough/research/bilgiler-emin-semsug/">Bilgiler page</a>
          carries it with a date of 8 December 2009. So this entry is most
          likely a shortened version of that longer text, or of whatever it
          came from. None of them names a source, and I haven’t found one.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>not independent.</strong> Because the wording and the
          order of facts match, these three web pages are one account, not three.
          The life dates (1900–1952) and the birthplace (Mansure) still conflict
          with other sources on this site, as set out on the Circassian Center
          page.
        </mark>
      </li>
      <li>
        <mark>
          Review: <strong>spelling.</strong> It uses “Semsuğ” throughout, unlike
          the longer text, which also uses “Semguğ” for his father.
        </mark>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
